# Secondary Sidebar UI & Spacing Conventions

This document outlines the standard layout, semantic elements, and pixel-perfect spacing guidelines for building secondary-column navigation sidebars in all modules.

---

## 1. The Spacing System & Alignment

Sidebar horizontal alignments are governed strictly by the following tokens in `@engenty/ui-core`:

| Token | CSS Class | Pixel Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `sidebarColumnGutterClassName` | `px-2` | **8px** (Left/Right) | Outer gutter applied on the parent column shell wrapper |
| `sidebarColumnContentInsetClassName` | `pl-2` | **8px** (Left) | Visual offset stacking on top of the gutter to hit a **16px** alignment grid |
| `sidebarColumnContentInsetEndClassName` | `pr-1` | **4px** (Right) | Inset to clear the column resize handle |

### Visual Alignments Grid

- **Search Inputs / Borders**: Must start at exactly **16px** left margin (8px gutter + `pl-2`).
- **Row Hover Backgrounds**: Must start at exactly **8px** left margin (aligning directly to the column gutter edge).
- **Icons**: Must sit at exactly **16px** left margin (8px gutter + 8px row inline padding).
- **Iconless List Items (Leaf items)**: Text must start at exactly **16px** left margin (8px gutter + 8px row inline padding).
- **Group Headings**: Text must start at exactly **16px** left margin.

---

## 2. Component Structure and Orchestration

Every module's sidebar panel should be split into a lightweight parent orchestrator and dedicated sub-components under its `components/` directory:

```
SidebarPanel (e.g. ProjectsSidebarPanel)
├── SidebarHeader (e.g. ProjectsSidebarHeader)
├── SidebarContent (Scrollable area)
│   └── SidebarList (e.g. ProjectsSidebarGroupedList)
└── SidebarFooter (e.g. ProjectsSidebarFooter)
```

### 1. Parent Orchestrator (e.g. `ProjectsSidebarPanel`)
- Owns all hooks, react-query hooks, search state, preferences (`sortBy`, `groupBy`), and modal triggers.
- Passes clean data down to the sub-components.
- Root element has `className="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col overflow-hidden"`.

---

## 3. Header Component Spacing (e.g. `ProjectsSidebarHeader`)

The header component manages the search row, filters, and top-level navigation rows.

### Search Row & Buttons
The search bar container must use the `pl-2 pr-1` wrapper offsets to hit the **16px** left border alignment:

```tsx
<div className="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1 pl-2 pr-1">
  <div className="relative min-w-0 flex-1">
    <Search className="pointer-events-none absolute top-1/2 left-2 size-3.5 -translate-y-1/2 text-muted-foreground/70" />
    <Input
      className="h-8 w-full py-0 pr-7 pl-8 text-sm"
      placeholder="Search..."
    />
  </div>
  {/* Filter buttons / Actions */}
</div>
```

### Main Nav Link Rows (e.g., "Projects", "Briefing")
- Do **not** wrap these inside a `<nav>` tag. Wrapping navigation list items inside nested `<nav>` tags is semantically redundant and pollutes the screen-reader accessibility tree (often duplicating labels from search inputs).
- Mount `<SidebarNavList>` directly under `<SidebarHeader>`.
- Set `className="pt-2"` on `<SidebarNavList>` to separate it from the search container.

```tsx
{isSearching ? null : (
  <SidebarNavList className="pt-2">
    <SidebarNavRow
      active={pathname === "/mdl/projects"}
      icon={DockProjectsIcon}
      label="Projects"
      to="/mdl/projects"
    />
  </SidebarNavList>
)}
```

---

## 4. Main Content Spacing (e.g. `ProjectsSidebarList`)

The main list renders groups and list items.

- Wrap scrollable areas with `<SidebarContent className="min-h-0 flex-1 gap-0.5 overflow-x-hidden px-0 py-0">`.
- Use a `SidebarGroup` with `p-0` inside the scroll container.
- **Do not apply `pl-2` or inset wrapper classes on list lists/groups.** `SidebarRow` automatically applies inline `paddingLeft: 8px` (when depth is `0`). Since the parent column has an 8px gutter, this automatically places the item text/icon at **16px** left.

```tsx
export function ProjectsSidebarGroupedList({ groups, renderItem }) {
  return (
    <SidebarNavList>
      {groups.map((group) => (
        <div className="contents" key={group.key}>
          {group.label ? (
            <SidebarNavSectionLabel>
              {group.label} ({group.projects.length})
            </SidebarNavSectionLabel>
          ) : null}
          {group.projects.map((item) => renderItem(item))}
        </div>
      ))}
    </SidebarNavList>
  );
}
```

---

## 5. Footer Component Spacing (e.g. `ProjectsSidebarFooter`)

The footer component is pinned to the bottom and holds the settings navigation.

- Wrapped in a `div` with `shrink-0 border-border/50 border-t pt-2 pr-1 pb-2` (removes left padding `pl-2` to align hover borders).
- Do **not** wrap in a `<nav>` tag.
- Mount `<SidebarNavList>` directly inside.

```tsx
export function ProjectsSidebarFooter({ pathname }) {
  return (
    <div className="shrink-0 border-border/50 border-t pt-2 pr-1 pb-2">
      <SidebarNavList>
        <SidebarNavRow
          active={pathname === "/mdl/projects/settings"}
          icon={Settings}
          label="Settings"
          to="/mdl/projects/settings"
        />
      </SidebarNavList>
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## 6. How SidebarRow and Padding Interact

`SidebarRow` (from `@engenty/ui-core`) handles its horizontal indentation based on the `depth` prop:
- `depth={0}` -> `paddingLeft` is inline **8px**.
- `depth={n}` -> `8px + n * 12px` per nested tier.

This is why **no parent wrapper around a list** should apply `pl-2` (which adds `8px`). Adding a `pl-2` wrapper on a list creates a **double-indent** (8px wrapper + 8px inline = 16px hover offset, throwing text/icons to 24px+).

---

## 7. Rebuilding Modules After UI Changes

Since modules are bundled in dev using `tsup` and consumed by the app shell from `dist/`, any changes to sidebar UI code must be built to become visible in the browser:

```bash
pnpm --filter @engenty/<module-name> build
```
*(Example: `pnpm --filter @engenty/projects build`)*
